		  <h2 data-swiftype-index=3D"false" class=3D"firstH2" id=3D"use">Calculat=
or Use</h2>
                <!-- InstanceBeginEditable name=3D"content" -->
                <p>The Least Common Multiple (<abbr title=3D"least common m=
ultiple">LCM</abbr>) is also referred to as the Lowest Common Multiple (<ab=
br title=3D"lowest common multiple">LCM</abbr>) and Least Common Divisor (<=
abbr title=3D"least common divisor">LCD)</abbr>. For two integers a and b, =
denoted LCM(a,b), the LCM is the smallest positive integer that is evenly d=
ivisible by both a and b. For example, LCM(2,3) =3D 6 and LCM(6,10) =3D 30.
                </p>
                <p>The LCM of two or more numbers is the smallest number th=
at is evenly divisible by all numbers in the set.</p>
                <h2>Least Common Multiple Calculator</h2>
                <p>Find the LCM of a set of numbers with this calculator wh=
ich also shows the steps and how to do the work.</p>
                <p>Input the numbers you want to find the LCM for. You can =
use commas or spaces to separate your numbers. But do not use commas within=
 your numbers. For example, enter
                    <strong>2500, 1000</strong> and not <strong>2,500, 1,00=
0</strong>.</p>
                <br>
                <h2>How to Find the Least Common Multiple LCM</h2>
                <p>This LCM calculator with steps finds the LCM and shows t=
he work using 6 different methods:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Listing Multiples</li>
                    <li>Prime Factorization</li>
                    <li>Cake/Ladder Method</li>
                    <li>Division Method</li>
                    <li>Using the Greatest Common Factor GCF</li>
                    <li>Venn Diagram</li>
                </ul>
                <br>
                <h2><span id=3D"list">How to Find LCM by Listing Multiples<=
/span></h2>
                <ul>
                    <li>List the multiples of each number until at least on=
e of the multiples appears on all lists</li>
                    <li>Find the smallest number that is on all of the list=
s</li>
                    <li>This number is the LCM</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Example: LCM(6,7,21)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Multiples of 6: 6, 12, 18, 24, 30, 36, <strong>42</=
strong>, 48, 54, 60</li>
                    <li>Multiples of 7: 7, 14, 21, 28, 35, <strong>42</stro=
ng>, 56, 63</li>
                    <li>Multiples of 21: 21, <strong>42</strong>, 63</li>
                    <li>Find the smallest number that is on all of the list=
s. We have it in bold above.</li>
                    <li>So LCM(6, 7, 21) is 42</li>
                </ul>
                <br>
                <h2 id=3D"primes">How to find LCM by Prime Factorization</h=
2>
                <ul>
                    <li>Find all the prime factors of each given number.</l=
i>
                    <li>List all the prime numbers found, as many times as =
they occur most often for any one given number.</li>
                    <li>Multiply the list of prime factors together to find=
 the LCM.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>The
                    <abbr title=3D"least common multiple">LCM</abbr>(a,b) i=
s calculated by finding the prime factorization of both a and b. Use the sa=
me process for the LCM of more than 2 numbers.
                </p>
                <p><strong>For example, for <abbr title=3D"least common mul=
tiple">LCM</abbr>(12,30) we find:</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Prime factorization of 12 =3D 2 =C3=97 2 =C3=97 3</=
li>
                    <li>Prime factorization of 30 =3D 2 =C3=97 3 =C3=97 5</=
li>
                    <li>Using all prime numbers found as often as each occu=
rs most often we take 2 =C3=97 2 =C3=97 3 =C3=97 5 =3D 60</li>
                    <li>Therefore <abbr title=3D"least common multiple">LCM=
</abbr>(12,30) =3D 60.</li>
                </ul>
                <p><strong>For example, for <abbr title=3D"least common mul=
tiple">LCM</abbr>(24,300) we find:</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Prime factorization of 24 =3D 2 =C3=97 2 =C3=97 2 =
=C3=97 3</li>
                    <li>Prime factorization of 300 =3D 2 =C3=97 2 =C3=97 3 =
=C3=97 5 =C3=97 5</li>
                    <li>Using all prime numbers found as often as each occu=
rs most often we take 2 =C3=97 2 =C3=97 2 =C3=97 3 =C3=97 5 =C3=97 5 =3D 60=
0</li>
                    <li>Therefore <abbr title=3D"least common multiple">LCM=
</abbr>(24,300) =3D 600.</li>
                </ul>
                <br>
                <h2>How to find LCM by Prime Factorization using Exponents<=
/h2>
                <ul>
                    <li>Find all the prime factors of each given number and=
 write them in exponent form.</li>
                    <li>List all the prime numbers found, using the highest=
 exponent found for each.</li>
                    <li>Multiply the list of prime factors with exponents t=
ogether to find the LCM.</li>
                </ul>
                                <p>Example: LCM(12,18,30)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Prime factors of 12 =3D 2 =C3=97 2 =C3=97 3 =3D 2<s=
up>2</sup> =C3=97 3<sup>1</sup></li>
                    <li>Prime factors of 18 =3D 2 =C3=97 3 =C3=97 3 =3D 2<s=
up>1</sup> =C3=97 3<sup>2</sup></li>
                    <li>Prime factors of 30 =3D 2 =C3=97 3 =C3=97 5 =3D 2<s=
up>1</sup> =C3=97 3<sup>1</sup> =C3=97 5<sup>1</sup>
                    </li>
                    <li>List all the prime numbers found, as many times as =
they occur most often for any one given number and multiply them together t=
o find the LCM
                        <ul>
                            <li>2 =C3=97 2 =C3=97 3 =C3=97 3 =C3=97 5 =3D 1=
80</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li>Using exponents instead, multiply together each of =
the prime numbers with the highest power
                        <ul>
                            <li>2<sup>2</sup> =C3=97 3<sup>2</sup> =C3=97 5=
<sup>1</sup> =3D 180</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li>So LCM(12,18,30) =3D 180</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Example: LCM(24,300)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Prime factors of 24 =3D 2 =C3=97 2 =C3=97 2 =C3=97 =
3 =3D 2<sup>3</sup> =C3=97 3<sup>1</sup></li>
                    <li>Prime factors of 300 =3D 2 =C3=97 2 =C3=97 3 =C3=97=
 5 =C3=97 5 =3D 2<sup>2</sup> =C3=97 3<sup>1</sup> =C3=97 5<sup>2</sup>
                    </li>
                    <li>List all the prime numbers found, as many times as =
they occur most often for any one given number and multiply them together t=
o find the LCM
                        <ul>
                            <li>2 =C3=97 2 =C3=97 2 =C3=97 3 =C3=97 5 =C3=
=97 5 =3D 600</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li>Using exponents instead, multiply together each of =
the prime numbers with the highest power
                        <ul>
                            <li>2<sup>3</sup> =C3=97 3<sup>1</sup> =C3=97 5=
<sup>2</sup> =3D 600</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li>So LCM(24,300) =3D 600</li>
                </ul>
                <br>
                <h2><span id=3D"cake">How to Find LCM Using the Cake Method=
 (Ladder Method)</span></h2>
                <p>The cake method uses division to find the LCM of a set o=
f numbers. People use the cake or ladder method as the fastest and easiest =
way to find the LCM because it is simple division. </p>
                <p>The cake method is the same as the ladder method, the bo=
x method, the factor box method and the grid method of shortcuts to find th=
e LCM. The boxes and grids might look a little different, but they all use =
division by primes to find LCM.</p>
                <p>Find the LCM(10, 12, 15, 75)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Write down your numbers in a cake layer (row)</li>
                </ul>
                <div class=3D"table cakeTable centered">
                    <div class=3D"caption">Cake&nbsp;/&nbsp;Ladder</div>
                    <div class=3D"row">
                        <div class=3D"cell bold alignRight">10</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell bold alignRight">12</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell bold alignRight">15</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell bold alignRight">75</div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <ul>
                    <li>Divide the layer numbers by a prime number that is =
evenly divisible into two or more numbers in the layer and bring down the r=
esult into the next layer.</li>
                </ul>
                <div class=3D"table cakeTable centered">
                    <div class=3D"caption">Cake&nbsp;/&nbsp;Ladder</div>
                    <div class=3D"row">
                        <div class=3D"cell cake_L alignCenter boldRight">2<=
/div>
                        <div class=3D"cell bold alignRight">10</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell bold alignRight">12</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell bold alignRight">15</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell bold alignRight">75</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class=3D"row">
                        <div class=3D"cell cake_L alignCenter">&nbsp;</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">5</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">6</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">&nbsp;</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">&nbsp;</div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <ul>
                    <li>If any number in the layer is not evenly divisible =
just bring down that number.</li>
                </ul>
                <div class=3D"table cakeTable centered">
                    <div class=3D"caption">Cake&nbsp;/&nbsp;Ladder</div>
                    <div class=3D"row">
                        <div class=3D"cell cake_L alignCenter boldRight">2<=
/div>
                        <div class=3D"cell bold alignRight">10</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell bold alignRight">12</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell bold alignRight">15</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell bold alignRight">75</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class=3D"row">
                        <div class=3D"cell cake_L alignCenter">&nbsp;</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">5</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">6</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">15</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">75</div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <ul>
                    <li>Continue dividing cake layers by prime numbers.</li=
>
                    <li>When there are no more primes that evenly divided i=
nto two or more numbers you are done.</li>
                </ul>
                <div class=3D"table cakeTable centered">
                    <div class=3D"caption">Cake&nbsp;/&nbsp;Ladder</div>
                    <div class=3D"row">
                        <div class=3D"cell cake_L alignCenter boldRight">2<=
/div>
                        <div class=3D"cell bold alignRight">10</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell bold alignRight">12</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell bold alignRight">15</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell bold alignRight">75</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class=3D"row">
                        <div class=3D"cell cake_L alignCenter boldRight">3<=
/div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">5</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">6</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">15</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">75</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class=3D"row">
                        <div class=3D"cell cake_L alignCenter boldRight">5<=
/div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">5</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">2</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">5</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">25</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class=3D"row">
                        <div class=3D"cell cake_L alignCenter"></div>
                        <div class=3D"cell cake_L alignRight boldTop">1</di=
v>
                        <div class=3D"cell cake_L alignRight boldTop">2</di=
v>
                        <div class=3D"cell cake_L alignRight boldTop">1</di=
v>
                        <div class=3D"cell cake_L alignRight boldTop">5</di=
v>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <ul>
                    <li>The LCM is the product of the numbers in the L shap=
e, left column and bottom row. 1 is ignored.</li>
                    <li>LCM =3D 2 =C3=97 3 =C3=97 5 =C3=97 2 =C3=97 5</li>
                    <li>LCM =3D 300</li>
                    <li>Therefore, LCM(10, 12, 15, 75) =3D 300</li>
                </ul>
                                <br>
                <h2><span id=3D"division">How to Find the LCM Using the Div=
ision Method</span></h2>
                <p>Find the LCM(10, 18, 25)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Write down your numbers in a top table row</li>
                </ul>
                <div class=3D"table prettytable centered">
                    <div class=3D"caption">Division&nbsp;Table</div>
                    <div class=3D"row">
                        <div class=3D"cell headerCell alignRight boldRight"=
>&nbsp;</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight">10</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight">18</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight">25</div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <ul>
                    <li>Starting with the lowest prime numbers, divide the =
row of numbers by a prime number that is evenly divisible into at least one=
 of your numbers and bring down the result into the next table row.</li>
                </ul>
                <div class=3D"table prettytable centered">
                    <div class=3D"caption">Division&nbsp;Table</div>
                    <div class=3D"row">
                        <div class=3D"cell headerCell alignRight boldRight"=
>2</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight">10</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight">18</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight">25</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class=3D"row">
                        <div class=3D"cell headerCell alignRight">&nbsp;</d=
iv>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">5</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">9</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">&nbsp;</div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <ul>
                    <li>If any number in the row is not evenly divisible ju=
st bring down that number.</li>
                </ul>
                <div class=3D"table prettytable centered">
                    <div class=3D"caption">Division&nbsp;Table</div>
                    <div class=3D"row">
                        <div class=3D"cell headerCell alignRight boldRight"=
>2</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight">10</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight">18</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight">25</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class=3D"row">
                        <div class=3D"cell headerCell alignRight">&nbsp;</d=
iv>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">5</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">9</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">25</div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <ul>
                    <li>Continue dividing rows by prime numbers that divide=
 evenly into at least one number.</li>
                    <li>When the last row of results is all 1's you are don=
e.</li>
                </ul>
                <div class=3D"table prettytable centered">
                    <div class=3D"caption">Division&nbsp;Table</div>
                    <div class=3D"row">
                        <div class=3D"cell headerCell alignRight boldRight"=
>2</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight">10</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight">18</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight">25</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class=3D"row">
                        <div class=3D"cell headerCell alignRight boldRight"=
>3</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">5</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">9</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">25</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class=3D"row">
                        <div class=3D"cell headerCell alignRight boldRight"=
>3</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">5</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">3</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">25</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class=3D"row">
                        <div class=3D"cell headerCell alignRight boldRight"=
>5</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">5</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">1</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">25</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class=3D"row">
                        <div class=3D"cell headerCell alignRight boldRight"=
>5</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">1</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">1</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">5</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class=3D"row">
                        <div class=3D"cell headerCell alignRight"></div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">1</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">1</div>
                        <div class=3D"cell alignRight boldTop">1</div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <ul>
                    <li>The LCM is the product of the prime numbers in the =
first column.</li>
                    <li>LCM =3D 2 =C3=97 3 =C3=97 3 =C3=97 5 =C3=97 5</li>
                    <li>LCM =3D 450</li>
                    <li>Therefore, LCM(10, 18, 25) =3D 450</li>
                </ul>
                                <br>
                <h2><span id=3D"gcf">How to Find LCM by GCF</span></h2>
                <p>The formula to find the LCM using the Greatest Common Fa=
ctor GCF of a set of numbers is:</p>
                <p>LCM(a,b) =3D (a=C3=97b)/GCF(a,b)</p>
                <p>Example: Find LCM(6,10)
                </p><ul>
                    <li>Find the GCF(6,10) =3D 2</li>
                    <li>Use the LCM by GCF formula to calculate (6=C3=9710)=
/2 =3D 60/2 =3D 30</li>
                    <li>So LCM(6,10) =3D 30</li>
                </ul>
                <p>A factor is a number that results when you can evenly di=
vide one number by another. In this sense, a factor is also known as a divi=
sor.</p>
                <p>The greatest common factor of two or more numbers is the=
 largest number shared by all the factors.</p>
                <h3>The greatest common factor GCF is the same as:</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>HCF - Highest Common Factor</li>
                    <li>GCD - Greatest Common Divisor</li>
                    <li>HCD - Highest Common Divisor</li>
                    <li>GCM - Greatest Common Measure</li>
                    <li>HCM - Highest Common Measure</li>
                </ul>
                <br>
                <h2><span id=3D"venn">How to Find the LCM Using Venn Diagra=
ms</span></h2>
                <p>Venn diagrams are drawn as overlapping circles. They are=
 used to show common elements, or intersections, between 2 or more objects.=
 In using Venn diagrams to find the LCM, prime factors of each number, we c=
all the groups, are distributed among overlapping circles to show the inter=
sections of the groups. Once the Venn diagram is completed you can find the=
 LCM by finding the union of the elements shown in the diagram groups and m=
ultiplying them together.</p>
                <br>
                <h2><span id=3D"LCMdecimals">How to Find LCM of Decimal Num=
bers</span></h2>
                <ul>
                    <li>Find the number with the most decimal places</li>
                    <li>Count the number of decimal places in that number. =
Let's call that number D.</li>
                    <li>For each of your numbers move the decimal D places =
to the right. All numbers will become integers.</li>
                    <li>Find the LCM of the set of integers</li>
                    <li>For your LCM, move the decimal D places to the left=
. This is the LCM for your original set of decimal numbers.</li>
                </ul>
                <br>
                <h2><span id=3D"properties">Properties of <abbr title=3D"le=
ast common multiple">LCM</abbr></span></h2>
                <h3>The LCM is associative: </h3>
                <p>LCM(a, b) =3D LCM(b, a) </p>
                <h3>The LCM is commutative: </h3>
                <p>LCM(a, b, c) =3D LCM(LCM(a, b), c) =3D LCM(a, LCM(b, c))=
</p>
                <h3>The LCM is distributive: </h3>
                <p>LCM(da, db, dc) =3D dLCM(a, b, c)</p>
                <h3>The LCM is related to the greatest common factor (GCF):=
 </h3>
                <p>LCM(a,b) =3D a =C3=97 b / GCF(a,b) and</p>
                <p>GCF(a,b) =3D a =C3=97 b / LCM(a,b) </p>
          <!--
          <h2>Least Common Factor (LCF)</h2>
          <p>The term "least common factor" is not a term used in mathemati=
